@@ -0,0 +1,28 @@
+Daily Grind uses native declarative partial updates with bundled
+`template-for-polyfill` and `html-setters-polyfill` for older browsers.
+Run `pnpm install` and `npm run build`; deploy `dist/` as the site root.
+The source root also works after building. No development server is required.
+
+The initial response contains the seasonal skeleton range followed by its
+`template for` patch. A production server can flush the shell before producing
+the patch; a static host delivers the same document without an artificial delay.
+
+Server integration:
+
+- `/partials/menu.html` returns fresh coffee card HTML with `Content-Type: text/html`.
+  The refresh button fetches it without caching. The provided file is a static
+  example; connect this route to the menu datastore for live menu updates.
+- `/api/menu/events` is an optional SSE endpoint (`text/event-stream`). Emit
+  `event: menu-changed` followed by `data: {}` and a blank line after a menu
+  change. The client also accepts `document.dispatchEvent(new Event('menu-changed'))`.
+  Static hosting has no SSE endpoint; the refresh button remains available.
+- `/partials/reviews.html` contains customer-submitted HTML. It always uses safe
+  setters, with DOMPurify when the browser lacks `setHTML` and the Sanitizer API.
+- `/partials/promo.html` must be exclusively trusted first-party CMS output. Its
+  unsafe streaming setter explicitly enables embedded widget scripts. Never put
+  customer input in this endpoint. With Trusted Types enforcement, configure an
+  audited policy for this trusted CMS path before allowing executable HTML.
+
+Native streaming setters render incrementally. The setters polyfill buffers
+until the stream completes. The static sample includes a review and CMS widget
+so both paths work without a backend.
